[Music]  today i'm  going to talk about feminism um  funny enough over the last few years  feminism has  taken such central stage in political  debates  and it hasn't been talked about for a  while because we're all rather obsessed  with  rightly so um the current pandemic and  larger political questions whether it be  about brexit or the economy  so the issue of feminism has sort of  taken a back seat  and um i want to look at the reasons why  i think the future of feminism is  something that we do need to talk about  and i think more importantly the future  of women's freedom is something that i  care a great deal about uh i think that  right from the off i'm going to come out  and say that  uh for me the future of feminism has to  be  a no future there can't be a future for  contemporary feminism as it currently  stands  um it can't carry on uh with the kind of  politics that's been running especially  for the last 10 years  whether it be from feminist campaigns  like the hashtag me2 movement  um or more centrally organized feminist  groups whether they be parliamentary  parties  or otherwise i think that feminism is  dead in the water i'm  not a feminist at myself and i wrote  about this in my book what women want  fun freedom  and an end to feminism which was quite a  long time ago it came out a number of  years ago  uh and you know made famous or at least  slightly made famous by mel  mel gibson was who i took my inspiration  from the book from but really because it  was trying to poke  a bit of fun at the sort of  uh religious nature of contemporary  feminism  at the moment you know the sort of  unequivocal expectation that women  should have to wear the f word um  as a label that that politicians feel  that they have to come out and state  that they are feminist celebrities  get more plaudits if they come out and  say that they have you know brandished  their feminist credentials  but actually um what i wanted to look at  when was writing this book and thinking  about feminism was  are most women feminists uh  and you know you can say what you like  about polling and say what you like  about  surveys they're often wrong but there's  have been  interesting numbers and data brought out  about the fact that very few women  actually label themselves feminists in  the uk anyway  it's somewhere between the 7 and nine  percent mark and that's coming from  surveys done by the fawcett society  which itself is a feminist organization  so stop your average woman on the street  and ask her what her political  identification is and it's  very unlikely that she'll say feminist  and  that i think is a problem i think that  contemporary feminism  uh as it stands is more likely to serve  the interests of a very small section of  society middle class women professionals  uh professional feminists i like to call  them people for whom  wearing that f badge that f word  uh gets them up and allows them to  socially climb  allows them with a wink and a nudge to  be accepted in the right kind of circles  but for the majority of women it does  very little other than you know become  something that  is on a pink glittery pencil case you  might buy your daughter  in primark or something that their  favorite celebrity might say when they  before they start their show and i think  that  part of the problem of this is that uh  feminism  has centrally become about fear the  politics of fear  and i'm going to look at three ways in  which that's that's come about  fear of work fear of sex and fear in  ourselves  and i think you know one of the things  that that part the problems of  contemporary feminism  is that while it seeks to portray itself  as  being about preventing fear for women so  we do have a lot of discussion around  what we can do to  make women more empowered to know what  language we can use  in the workplace or elsewhere to you  know make  women stronger to uh you know to protect  us against  negative or sexist stereotypes and  underpinning  a lot of the conversation around what  women what feminists seem to think women  need  is uh around a politics of fear so we're  told that  the world outside our four walls is  wrapped with  uh fearful things whether it be sexist  bosses  sexist partners sexist family members  the sexist man waiting outside the  corner shop  and also within our own homes that  actually that we are sort of brought up  shrouded in fear worried about our body  image worried about our sense of self  worried about whether or not we'll fit  in worried about whether or not we'll  fit in with our male  uh partners and relationships and all of  that i think there's a huge  amount of fear going on in the  in the politics of feminism ironically  at a time in which  the world is a far less fearful place  for women you know even in the last 30  years the kind of  changes that women themselves have made  in through political campaigns  and fights for freedom has meant that  really for women living in the west  certainly in the uk  there is very little to fear and so i  want to kind of look at why  given the situation we're in that women  have sort of actually never had it so  good  that we are expected to be afraid of  everything so i want to first  take a look at the fear of work  and fear in the workplace because this  is something that keeps coming up so  if you are a young woman about to go out  into the  workplace whether it be in office or  anywhere else the office is often where  it's  this kind of discussion centers around  which in itself might give you an  indication of how limited some of the  the scope of some of the politics of  feminism  is given that not all women work in  offices but sticking with the office  uh sort of example the first thing that  you're told if you pay any attention to  what your  uh life might be like when you're when  you go out into work  is this question of the pay gap you're  told that as a woman you are  categorically not going to earn as much  as men  that the odds are stacked against you  and that you know  essentially don't don't think about  trying to kind of be ambitious around  this because ever  you know there's sexism inbuilt into the  system  and when you try and pick apart gender  pay gap data you're  quite often told that you're sort of  denying reality that you are  um being sort of oblivious to to the  facts and obviously the  part of the problem is that the data  around this is very complicated and i've  just sort of tried to be a bit  piss-taking here by  pulling out several headlines and  showing that even amongst themselves  people who agree with the  sort of official feminist line that  women are that the gender pay gap is  real  that women are discriminated against in  the workplace they don't always agree so  we've got you know it's going to take  60 years to eradicate the gap 250 years  to close the gap  you know 99.5 years to close the gap  every year  it changes not in relation to any kind  of material reality  but because a lot of the time the stats  involved in uh  how you assess women's um place in  payrolls and  how you assess the way women are are  paid in comparison to their male  counterparts is always based on  averages and joanna williams does a  great uh deal of work on this  she's a a writer and commentator  pointing out the fact that  actually the things that don't get  talked about is the fact that women in  their 20s and 30s are often out earning  men but more are going to university and  that we're doing really quite well  the difficult part and the way the way  in which women do suffer when it comes  to pay  is when they begin to reach a period  when they want to have children  that's when pay differences come in in  terms of  and women not being offered the same  kind of full-time work or not being  offered the same kind of salary after  they have children but the idea that  simply  being a woman and entering the workplace  and this at the same day as a man and  you're being discriminated against  there isn't the data to show that at  least it's illegal  but there just is not the data to show  that so the question is why is this  something  that we're constantly told we should be  frightened of and then obviously the  other  massive thing that's been that's sort of  given women  uh the need to be fearful in the  workplace is this discussion around  hashtag me too which i'll come to a bit  when i'm talking about sex but  the idea of the office um around the  water cooler  you know if they even exist anymore  around the workbench that you should be  always on your guard for sexist remarks  from bosses  sexist remarks from colleagues that  women need to have policies instituted  zero tolerance policies  put in place in their workplaces to  prevent them from  uh having to deal with any kind of  negative behavior from  men and again this is a kind of a  completely out of touch uh picture of  the fact that actually  most women might in some cases  have to deal with negative behavior in  the office from men and women and from  all different kinds of hardships  but the idea that you would need a  patronizing policy  to prevent any kind of flirtatious  behavior or some of the things that hit  the headlines over the last few  years in relation to hashtag me too is  stopping drinking at work christmas  parties in case  people take advantage of drunk women  because  drunk women don't ever want to have any  fun in the christmas party uh  or that you need to have special in  policies instituted like chaperones  where if a woman is having an interview  with a man they shouldn't be left alone  she should be  given a chaperone and it's these kinds  of extremely patronizing actually in  some cases rather sexist  protective policies i'll feed into this  idea  that the workplace is somewhere that  women should really be fearful of and  should always be on their guard of  what i'm going to come to later is you  know talking about the fact of why that  is so negative because if you are going  into there's no one who ever went into  um you know a meeting about increasing  their pay  or getting a better role fit  frightful that their boss is going to  touch them uh you know  on the knee under the table ever got  anything out of that so constantly  filling women with  fear about these kind of interactions is  only going to limit their ability to act  as  as individual agents and as sort of  quote unquote strong  empowered women to continue watching  this video  click the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai dot tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas

